format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-meets-matter

objective u3-background-reading
statement: Know that a radiation detector picks up some radiation almost anywhere, because background radiation is always present.
misconception: A detector that picks up radiation must be right next to a dangerous source.

item u3-l4-choice-background
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

prompt:
    Why does a working detector read a little in an empty field?

options:
    {"id":"broken","label":"It is broken","note":"A working detector reads a little almost anywhere, because background radiation is always present."}
    {"id":"bg","label":"Background radiation is always present on Earth"}
    {"id":"leak","label":"There is a hidden source close by","note":"No source is needed. Background radiation comes from natural minerals in the ground, soil and water, and from cosmic radiation."}

answer: bg

explanation:
    Background radiation is present on Earth at all times. It comes from natural minerals and from cosmic radiation, so a working detector picks up a little almost anywhere.

item u3-l4-choice-sources
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

prompt:
    Where does background radiation mostly come from?

options:
    {"id":"phones","label":"Phones and radios","note":"Background radiation is natural. It comes mostly from minerals in the ground, soil and water, and from cosmic radiation."}
    {"id":"nat","label":"Natural minerals in the ground, soil and water, and cosmic radiation"}
    {"id":"plants","label":"Power plants only","note":"It is present everywhere on Earth, and comes mostly from natural sources."}

answer: nat

explanation:
    Background radiation is mostly natural: minerals in the ground, soil and water, and cosmic radiation from space.

item u3-l4-tom-source
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

statement:
    Cosmic radiation is part of background radiation.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. Along with natural minerals in the ground, soil and water, cosmic radiation is one of the main sources of background radiation.

item u3-l4-tom-zero
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

statement:
    A detector that reads above zero must be right next to a dangerous source.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. A working detector picks up background radiation almost anywhere. A small reading is normal, and it varies from place to place.

item u3-x-background-reading
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

prompt:
    A detector reads a few counts in two different places, and the numbers are not the same. What is the most likely reason?

options:
    {"id":"vary","label":"Background radiation varies from place to place"}
    {"id":"broken","label":"One of the detectors is broken","note":"Nothing needs to be broken. Background radiation varies from place to place, so readings differ."}
    {"id":"nonsense","label":"Detectors only ever give random numbers","note":"A working detector reflects the radiation that is there, and that varies by place."}

answer: vary

explanation:
    Background radiation is not the same everywhere on Earth, so a working detector gives different readings in different places.

item u3-x-tom-background-natural
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

statement:
    Background radiation is present on Earth at all times.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. It comes mostly from natural sources, so it is always there, and a detector picks up a little of it almost anywhere.
